WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.475 --> 00:00:06.850
CHRIS LATIMER:
Here at Google, we

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.150
continue to see a lot of
developers building their APIs

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:11.920
on top of microservices.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.260
So in this example, we've
deployed a weather API

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:15.430
on Cloud Run.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:18.310
It's made up of four
different services.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.260
This is great for you
as an API provider

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.090
because it gives you
a lot of flexibility

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:25.520
around how you deploy and
how you scale these services.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.100
But as an API
consumer, you don't

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.570
want to keep track
of which service

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.630
is responsible for
which piece of the API.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.130
You just want one
interface that you

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.620
can call to access all
the capabilities provided

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.490
by this API.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.510
So let's take a look at how
Google Cloud's API gateway can

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.330
help you combine all
these services into one

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.130
easy-to-use API.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.730
To get started, we'll need
an OpenAPI specification

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.830
that defines each of the
resources in our API.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:52.450
Next, we'll add
instructions that

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.090
tell the API gateway where
to route the request for each

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.010
of those resources.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:58.780
Finally, we'll want
to secure our API,

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.970
so we'll configure a
security definition

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:05.269
that requires all incoming
calls to provide a valid API.

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:07.280
Once we have our
OpenAPI specification

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.830
and we're ready to
create our API gateway,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.750
first we'll specify
the display name

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.660
and give our API a
unique identifier.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.480
Next, we'll define the
API's initial configuration

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:18.860
by uploading our OpenAPI.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.390
File.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:23.120
We'll then give our
configuration a display name,

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:24.980
and we'll configure
the service account

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.400
that will be used to call
our back-end services.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.070
Finally, we're ready to
deploy our API configuration

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.660
to a new gateway.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:36.420
In this case, that gateway will
run in the US Central region.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.430
Once we hit Create,
after a few minutes,

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.400
our gateway will be ready to
start accepting incoming API

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.940
requests.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.880
Because we've secured our API,
we'll need valid credentials.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.730
In this case, we've elected to
use API keys as our security

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.350
mechanism.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.440
We can get an API key
for our API and the APIs

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.260
in Services section of
the Google Cloud console.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.960
Once we have our key,
we can make an API call

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.300
from the command
line using Chrome.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.120
If we try to access our API
without providing a key,

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.550
the call will be
rejected by the gateway.

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.229
Once we add our
key to the request,

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:12.500
the gateway then allows our
call to go through successfully.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.000
That was just a
quick preview of how

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.370
you can use the new API gateway
to create and secure your APIs.

